# The mutex contract: a free lock's invariant owns its cell's permission and
# the futex mutex hands it to one holder at a time. In the fork window an
# acquire the window serves (fork_holder_is_me) also takes a permission,
# one that stands for the fork handlers' hold of a lock prepare took (no
# arena, and so no lock, is built in the window), used by the forking
# thread alone until the release.
# loom (verus/WEAKMEM.md, P2): the hand-off rests on `RawLock`'s mutual
# exclusion and on its acquire/release edge (from the second acquisition
# on, `try_acquire`'s and `lock_slow`'s `Acquire` exchanges read the
# `Release` swap of the previous holder's `release`, so its writes to the
# cell happen before the next holder's reads; the first holder's view of
# the initial value is ordered by the lock's publication, not by this
# edge). The lock models check both, on a copy of
# `RawLock` with a futex stand-in: two holders at once, or a holder that
# misses the previous one's write, is a race on the counter they bump.
axiom_acquired	allocator invariant	loom/GenMC	trusted (partly checked, loom: lock::loom_lock_two_threads and lock::loom_lock_three_threads; not checked: the permission hand-off itself, a fact of Verus's own model (that the one `PointsTo` the invariant holds while the lock is free, taken out at each acquisition, stands for the cell's contents under the edge the models check); the `lock_inv` and `lock_frame` clauses, which rest on the holders' releases (`LockGuard::unlock` and `Lock::unlock_held_perm` require them, a dropped guard's release does not), not on the atomics; at the first acquisition, the initial value's visibility, which rests on the lock's publication, not on the edge the models check, and, for a per-arena statistics lock, its initial value, which the trusted `Arena::put_stats` writes instead of `Lock::new` (whose `requires` checks `lock_inv` and `lock_frame` of the value with itself; a test compares the bytes with those of `Lock::new(ArenaStats::empty(index), ..)`, `ArenaStats::empty` ensures the invariant, `ArenaStats::identities`, and the frame is `true`); the call discipline (only right after an acquire, never while an earlier permission is alive), which `lock::tests::permission_axioms_are_called_only_at_acquisitions` backs by matching source text only; the fork window's second permission, which rests on that window's premise; and the shipped `RawLock`, which the models replace with a copy, with two lockers and with three bounded at 5 preemptions, and the kernel's futex, which they replace with a stand-in)	contract 3374465368, body none
# loom (verus/WEAKMEM.md, P2): `LockGuard::drop`'s body is
# `RawLock::release` (`swap(UNLOCKED, Release)`, then a futex wake when it
# returned `CONTENDED`); the lock models run a copy of `RawLock` with a
# futex stand-in and check mutual exclusion, the acquire/release edge and
# no lost wake-up.
LockGuard::drop	atomics primitive	loom/GenMC	trusted (partly checked, loom: lock::loom_lock_two_threads and lock::loom_lock_three_threads; not checked: this body, which the models replace with a copy of `RawLock::release`, with two lockers and with three bounded at 5 preemptions; the kernel's futex, which the models replace with a stand-in that also orders the waker's accesses before the woken thread's return)	contract 3065263068, body 2348458592
# `lock_slow`'s termination does not follow from the models: Drepper's
# mutex 3 is not starvation-free, so a woken waiter can lose the word to
# another thread's exchange as often as other threads retake the lock.
# The lock models show deadlock freedom (no lost wake-up between the
# `swap(CONTENDED)` and the sleep, nor for a sleeper behind another
# waiter) in their configurations (lock::loom_lock_two_threads, and
# lock::loom_lock_three_threads bounded at 5 preemptions, each locker
# taking the lock once, on a copy): evidence, not termination, which is
# all the contract claims, so the seam stays trusted.
RawLock::lock_slow	termination	loom/GenMC
# Lock-order premise of a C call: its caller holds no allocator lock.
# Minted only in export bodies, one per body
# (api::tests::c_call_tokens_only_in_export_bodies); Verus checks every
# use below the export.
axiom_c_entry	C-program contract	none (assumption)
# `Shared::init` holds no allocator lock: none exists before the `Shared`
# is published (lock::tests::root_lock_order_uses_are_listed pins the call).
axiom_init_entry	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# A thread's start routine and its exit key's destructor run with no
# allocator code on the stack below them (the background thread's `main`,
# `heap_destructor`; pinned by the same test).
axiom_thread_entry	platform/kernel fact	none (assumption)
# The fork prepare handler holds no lock at entry and keeps its token
# throughout. No allocator code forks and glibc runs the handler from
# `fork`, so a lock held at entry needs a `fork` from a signal handler
# that interrupted allocator code, which is outside the C contract (`fork`
# is not async-signal-safe). Verus checks the token's use (`lock_held`
# keeps the root).
axiom_fork_entry	C-program contract	none (assumption)
# The fork window (design 9): the forking thread, recorded by
# `fork_window_open` at the end of the prepare handler and cleared by
# `fork_window_close` before the release, takes the held locks as
# acquired and releases nothing. Premise: it has no allocator critical
# section open in the window, so each lock's data is as its last holder
# left it and only that thread runs allocator code until the release.
# What the fork handlers snapshot or reset does not change in the window
# (the background thread's start is refused there: in_fork_window). Not
# covered: a process `_Fork`ed or cloned inside the window inherits the
# record as its own (design 9, residuals). No arena is built in the
# window (`Arenas::create` returns None, so `arenas.create` is EAGAIN;
# `Arenas::materialise` returns None, so an allocation whose home is not
# built is served from arena 0): every lock is one prepare took, the
# window serves only those, and the registry the release reads
# (`count_frozen`, `get_frozen`) is the one prepare locked.
# The window's record is verified code over two atomics (fork_holder_is_me,
# in_fork_window, in_child_fork_window, fork_window_open,
# fork_window_close); the premise above
# rests on axiom_acquired, and the thread's identity on sys::pthread_self.
# The child's single thread before challoc's child handler: what
# `in_fork_child` stands for, read from the window's record by
# `in_child_fork_window` (verified). Platform facts: `pthread_self` tells
# a process's live threads apart and (glibc) the forking thread keeps it
# in the child, the child starts with a copy of the parent's memory, and
# in one PID namespace its `getpid` is not the parent's. The record's
# writers, this use and its reads are
# pinned by lock::tests::fork_record_uses_are_listed (no loom model
# covers the in-window reset).
axiom_child_window	platform/kernel fact	none (assumption)
# A field projection, `requires perm.ptr() == p`, no `ensures`: the
# permission names the storage of a `Lock<T>` (vstd's `PointsTo` stands
# for live memory, aligned for its type), and the harness runs the body on
# such storage, at four `T`s. Its one caller, `Arena::put_stats`, calls
# it at `T = ArenaStats`, one of the harness's `T`s, from a trusted body
# Verus does not check: lock::tests::value_ptr_called_only_by_put_stats
# pins that it passes its own `p` and the permission its `requires` names.
Lock::value_ptr	raw pointer (internal)	Kani	discharged (Kani: lock::kani_lock::value_ptr_is_the_value_field)	contract 2722103989, body 2553620183
